What the Social Sciences Can Learn from Physics

Toward a More Mathematical, Predictive, and Unified Science of Human Society

The social sciences study human beings, institutions, economies, cultures, and political systems. Physics studies matter, energy, motion, and the fundamental laws governing physical systems.

Although their objects of study differ, physics offers the social sciences a powerful methodological lesson: a science should strive not merely to describe what happens, but to discover the mathematical structures that explain why it happens, predict what will happen under specified conditions, and identify when its predictions will fail.

Sociology, economics, political science, anthropology, and psychology can learn a great deal from the methods of physics. However, they must adapt these methods to a fundamental difference: human beings interpret their circumstances, change their behavior in response to predictions, and create institutions that can transform the very rules governing their lives.

Here are ten lessons the social sciences can draw from physics.

