7. Lesson Seven: Treat Causality as a Structural Question

Physics frequently investigates how systems respond when forces, fields, or boundary conditions change.

The social sciences face an analogous challenge: determining whether changing one variable causes a change in another.

Suppose students who attend a particular school have higher average test scores than students who attend another school.

Does the school cause the difference?

Not necessarily.

Students may differ in family income, prior achievement, neighborhood conditions, or access to educational resources.

A correlation alone does not isolate the causal effect of the school.

One useful framework is the potential-outcomes model.

Let:

Y_i(1)=\text{outcome for individual }i\text{ under treatment}

Y_i(0)=\text{outcome for individual }i\text{ without treatment}

The individual causal effect is:

\tau_i=Y_i(1)-Y_i(0)

The central problem is that we cannot ordinarily observe both potential outcomes for the same individual under the same circumstances.

Researchers therefore use randomized experiments, natural experiments, instrumental variables, regression discontinuity, difference-in-differences, and other methods to estimate causal effects under explicit assumptions.

Physics offers a useful intellectual ideal here: specify the intervention, isolate the relevant mechanism as far as possible, and determine what changes when the intervention occurs.

But social systems make this difficult because interventions can change the behavior of other people, alter institutions, and generate feedback effects.

A school reform, for example, might change not only student learning but also teacher incentives, parental decisions, and school enrollment patterns.

Consequently, the effect of an intervention on one individual may differ from its effect when applied to an entire population.

The social sciences should study both individual causal mechanisms and the system-wide effects of interventions.

