8. Lesson Eight: Make Models Falsifiable and Predictions Measurable

A scientific theory becomes much more useful when it rules out some possible observations.

If a theory can explain every conceivable outcome after the fact, it offers little predictive value.

Physics often produces precise quantitative predictions that can be compared with experiments.

Social science can adopt the same discipline.

Suppose a theory predicts that a particular tax change will increase employment by a measurable amount within three years.

Researchers should specify in advance:

* The predicted direction of the effect.
* The expected magnitude or plausible range.
* The population to which the prediction applies.
* The relevant time horizon.
* The conditions under which the prediction is expected to hold.
* The evidence that would count against the theory.

A prediction can then be assessed against observed outcomes.

One possible measure of predictive performance is mean squared error:

MSE=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}(y_i-\hat y_i)^2

Where:

* y_i is an observed outcome.
* \hat y_i is the predicted outcome.
* n is the number of observations.

A lower mean squared error indicates better performance on the evaluated observations, although the metric’s suitability depends on the task and the costs of different kinds of mistakes.

Researchers should also test their models on data not used to develop them.

This helps distinguish genuine predictive ability from overfitting.

For probabilistic forecasts, calibration matters too. If a model assigns a 70% probability to events across many comparable cases, those events should occur approximately 70% of the time for the forecasts to be well calibrated.

The objective is to reward theories that survive demanding tests, not theories that merely produce persuasive explanations.

