Toward a Unified Mathematical Science of Society

These lessons suggest a research program that combines methods from several disciplines.

Discipline	Contribution to the study of society
Classical mechanics and dynamical systems	Modeling change over time and identifying equilibria.
Statistical mechanics	Understanding aggregate patterns generated by many interacting individuals.
Thermodynamics	Investigating constraints, flows, and irreversible processes where the analogy is appropriate.
Network science	Modeling relationships, influence, and the spread of information.
Probability theory	Quantifying uncertainty and developing probabilistic forecasts.
Information theory	Measuring uncertainty and studying information transmission.
Control theory	Understanding feedback, stability, and intervention.
Game theory	Modeling strategic interactions between agents.
Econometrics	Estimating relationships and evaluating causal claims.
Agent-based modeling	Exploring how micro-level rules generate macro-level outcomes.

The unifying objective is to understand the relationship between individual behavior, system structure, and collective outcomes.

This does not require reducing every social phenomenon to a single equation. Different questions may require different models, scales of analysis, and standards of evidence.

The most productive approach is pluralistic: use the simplest model that captures the mechanism relevant to the question, then test whether it works.

