Final Thesis

The social sciences can learn from physics that rigorous scientific inquiry requires more than observation, description, and interpretation.

It requires precise concepts, explicit assumptions, mathematical models, causal reasoning, quantitative predictions, and systematic attempts to discover where those predictions fail.

Physics demonstrates the extraordinary explanatory power that mathematics can provide when combined with careful observation and experimentation.

Social science can pursue a comparable level of rigor while recognizing that human beings are strategic, self-interpreting agents embedded in institutions that change over time.

The ultimate ambition is not to discover a single equation governing every aspect of humanity.

It is to develop a collection of well-tested mathematical theories that explain how people interact, how institutions shape behavior, how collective patterns emerge, and how societies change.

The deepest lesson from physics is not that society must obey the same equations as the physical world. It is that our explanations of society should be precise enough to test, powerful enough to predict, and humble enough to be revised when reality disagrees.
